    "conclusion": "Care ethics provisionally recommends giving the blanket to Rowan because this is the more competent, life-preserving response when relational factors are equal, although a reasonable objection appealing to Sasha’s greater vulnerability remains.",
    "strongest_objection": "Sasha’s lower survival probability suggests greater fragility; a carer could prioritize the more vulnerable even when success chances are lower.",
    "answer_to_objection": "The competence-of-care norm gives decisive weight to the action that is more likely to save a life when other relationships are equal; here the 20-percentage-point difference is substantial, so responding to Rowan better fulfils the core caring aim of sustaining life.",
    "reversal_condition": "If new facts showed Sasha faced special relational ties to Ellis or Rowan’s higher probability were only marginal (e.g., 62% vs 60%), the vulnerability consideration could override and favor Sasha.",
    "residual_disagreement": [
      "Whether vulnerability should outweigh competence when the gap in success probability is significant"
    ]
  },
